  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two bodies of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> are connected by a light string going over a smooth light pulley on a smooth inclined plane as shown in the figure. The system is at rest. The force exerted by the inclined plane on the body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> will be : [Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>]</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0064_e792026f2413.png\" style=\"width: 279px; height: 120px;\" />​​​​​​​</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The forces acting on two masses are shown below.</p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;normal to the incline and tension&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;along the string up the incline.&nbsp;As the string and the pulley are all light and smooth, the tension in the string is uniform everywhere.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0064_6d70b5165543.png\" style=\"width: 329px; height: 270px;\" />​​​​​​​</p><p>For equilibrium condition, forces should add to zero.</p><p>Thus, equating,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>g</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>&theta;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&rArr;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>&theta;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>&theta;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Normal force on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>g</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&theta;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&theta;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}